#Since my Docker context is project-level (SmartMenuOptim.API), the following .dockerignore is optimized specifically for that folder. 
#It ensures only necessary files are sent to the Docker daemon, improving build performance and keeping secrets and dev files out of the image.

# Best Practices:
# Make sure you build from the folder where this .dockerignore lives (SmartMenuOptim.API) to take full advantage of its scoped exclusions.
# Avoid including sensitive files like appsettings.json in production builds unless explicitly required — instead, pass values via environment variables or secret managers.
# If you're using Dockerfile for development and Dockerfile.prod for production, you can selectively include/exclude them.


# Build artifacts
bin/
obj/
out/
TestResults/

# IDE/editor folders
.vs/
.vscode/
.idea/

# User-specific and temp files
*.suo
*.user
*.userosscache
*.sln.docstates
*.jfm

# Logs
*.log
npm-debug.log

# Secrets and config files (do not copy into image)
appsettings.*.Secrets.json
appsettings.Secrets.json
appsettings.Local.Secrets.json
appsettings.Development.Secrets.json
appsettings.json
secrets.dev.yaml
values.dev.yaml

# Docker and compose
.dockerignore
.docker/
docker-compose*
Dockerfile*

# Git
.git/
*.gitignore

# Package and dependency folders
node_modules/
packages/

# Local DB files
*.db
*.dbmdl

# Misc/IDE tools
.project
.settings/
.toolstarget
.classpath

# Azure Dev Spaces / Helm / Kubernetes
azds.yaml
charts/

# Environment files
.env

# Optional (non-build docs/licenses)
LICENSE
README.md